comments.body:
Unfortunately I don't think this is something that we can solve generically for you.

Any input type to an activity absolutely does have to be serializable. The activity inputs need to be sent to the server and back, and may run on a different worker entirely than the workflow itself. Because `ToolContext` is not serializable, it _cannot_ be an activity argument. You can take a look at https://github.com/temporalio/sdk-python/blob/0795a654977dc5c79cb55db7a41155a9d5ccccf8/temporalio/contrib/google_adk_agents/_mcp.py to see how we worked around this in the particular case of MCP tools.

What I would recommend is to explicitly call out the pieces of `ToolContext` which are actually needed by your tool. If those are serializable, you can populate those activity inputs from the tool context before activity invocation, though you won't be able to use `activity_tool` because that would add those inputs to the tool schema. Instead, you can define an adk tool function directly something like so: 

```
@activity.defn
def some_activity(something: T1, something_else: T2):
    pass

def some_tool(context: ToolContext):
    return await workflow.execute_activity(some_activity, args=[context.Something, context.SomethingElse])
```
(Though we actually recommend a single input object for extensibility if you need to add things)

If you need to _modify_ the tool context, those modifications need to happen inside the tool function, not the activity, because they are executing on different objects. You can return the information needed from the activity and use the result in the tool to do what is needed. This is what we do for approval of MCP tools.
